---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';

const title = 'PDR & Auto Hail Repair FAQ | Peak Country';
const description = 'Straight answers about paintless dent repair, auto hail damage, inspections, insurance-related repairs, mobile service, and hail reports in Northern Colorado.';
const repairFaqs = [
  ['What is Paintless Dent Repair?', 'Paintless Dent Repair, or PDR, is a method of gradually reshaping suitable dented metal while retaining the existing paint. A technician reads the panel under reflection lighting and uses controlled push or glue-pull techniques based on the dent, finish, material, and access.'],
  ['Can hail damage be repaired without repainting?', 'Yes, many hail dents can be repaired without repainting when the paint is intact and the metal remains suitable for PDR. Cracked paint, excessive stretch, severe impacts, or other conditions may require conventional repair.'],
  ['Does PDR preserve factory paint?', 'Yes, preserving the existing finish is a central benefit of PDR when the damage qualifies. Because the panel is reshaped without sanding, filler, or refinishing, the original paint remains in place.'],
  ['Can every hail dent be repaired with PDR?', 'No, not every hail dent qualifies for PDR. Paint condition, depth, sharpness, metal stretch, body-line location, panel material, previous repair, and safe access all affect repairability.'],
  ['Can PDR repair aluminum panels?', 'Yes, some aluminum-panel dents can be repaired with PDR. Aluminum behaves differently from steel, so the technician must evaluate the panel construction, damage, paint, and access before recommending the method.'],
  ['Can dents on body lines be repaired?', 'Yes, some body-line dents can be repaired with PDR. Formed character lines often carry more tension, which makes the dent’s depth, sharpness, finish condition, and location especially important.'],
  ['Can sharp dents or creases be repaired?', 'Sometimes, depending on the damage. Minor creases and certain sharp dents may respond to PDR, while pinched centers, excessive stretch, edge damage, or compromised paint can limit the result or require another method.'],
  ['Does cracked paint affect whether PDR can be used?', 'Yes, cracked paint can make a paintless repair inappropriate. PDR moves metal but does not restore a damaged coating, and further movement could affect an already compromised finish.'],
  ['How long does hail repair take?', 'The repair time varies with dent count, severity, affected panels, access, disassembly, parts or trim needs, insurer requirements, and scheduling. Peak Country discusses a realistic range after inspecting the vehicle and defining the scope.'],
  ['How do I know if my vehicle has hail damage?', 'Inspect the vehicle safely in clean, bright conditions and look for changes in reflected lines across the hood, roof, rails, doors, and other panels. Shallow dents are easy to miss, so professional reflection lighting provides a more reliable assessment.'],
  ['Should I have my vehicle inspected after a hailstorm?', 'Yes, an inspection is reasonable if the vehicle was exposed or you notice possible damage. A direct assessment can distinguish hail dents from unrelated wear and determine whether PDR is appropriate.'],
];
const insuranceFaqs = [
  ['Can Peak Country work with an insurance-related repair?', 'Yes. Peak Country can evaluate the damage, prepare repair estimates, document repair requirements, research applicable OEM procedures, prepare supplements, and provide supporting repair information for insurance-related hail, PDR, and collision repairs. Documentation can be provided to you, your insurance company, or another repair facility when appropriate.'],
  ['What if I already have an insurance estimate?', 'Bring it with you. An existing estimate can be a useful starting point, but it may not include every operation ultimately required to complete the repair. Peak Country can compare the estimate with the actual vehicle and review additional or previously undocumented damage, missing repair operations, R&I requirements, repair-method changes, OEM procedures and position statements, vehicle-specific technical information, and supplemental repair requirements. If additional documentation is needed, Peak Country can prepare a professional estimate review or supplement.'],
  ['Why might Peak Country’s estimate be different from my insurance estimate?', 'Estimate differences can occur for several legitimate reasons. The vehicle may have received a more detailed inspection, additional damage may have been discovered, disassembly may be required, or vehicle-specific repair information may identify additional operations or a different repair method. Peak Country documents what is being recommended and why so the repair plan can be evaluated based on the vehicle and repair requirements rather than only the final dollar amount.'],
  ['What is a supplement?', 'A supplement documents repair requirements that were not included in an earlier estimate or that became known after additional inspection, research, or disassembly. Peak Country can prepare supporting documentation when these differences are identified.'],
  ['Will Peak Country communicate with my insurance company?', 'When appropriate, yes. Peak Country can provide repair estimates, photographs, supplements, OEM information, and other repair-related documentation and can communicate about the vehicle damage and proposed repair. Our role is to accurately evaluate, research, and document the repair.'],
  ['Do I have to repair my vehicle with Peak Country?', 'No. Professional estimating and documentation services are available whether Peak Country ultimately performs the repair or you choose another qualified repair facility. If Peak Country performs the repair according to the documented repair plan, the applicable estimating fee is credited in full toward the repair.'],
  ['What about my insurance deductible?', 'Deductibles and other out-of-pocket responsibilities depend on the terms of your individual insurance policy. Peak Country keeps the repair plan separate from the deductible. Repair recommendations, required operations, and supporting documentation are based on what the vehicle needs rather than changing the repair plan simply to offset an insurance deductible. Pricing, repair options, and expected customer costs can be discussed before the repair is authorized.'],
  ['Does Peak Country guarantee that insurance will pay everything on the estimate?', 'No repair estimate can guarantee insurance coverage or reimbursement. Peak Country’s estimates and documentation are based on the damage identified, the proposed repair, applicable technical information, and the repair requirements discovered during evaluation. Insurance coverage, reimbursement, deductibles, limitations, and exclusions are subject to the terms of your individual insurance policy.'],
  ['Can Peak Country help if the proposed repair method does not look right?', 'Yes. Peak Country can review an existing hail or collision estimate and compare the proposed repair method with the actual vehicle damage, vehicle construction, applicable OEM information, and other repair requirements. When a different repair method or additional operation appears appropriate, Peak Country can document the reason for the recommendation and provide supporting technical information.'],
  ['What kind of technical information does Peak Country use?', 'Depending on the vehicle and repair, Peak Country may use professional estimating resources, commercial repair-information systems, OEM repair procedures, OEM position statements, labor and R&I information, vehicle-specific technical information, and other professional repair resources.'],
  ['Is the insurance estimate review free?', 'A basic discussion of your existing estimate can be part of the Free Initial Assessment. When a detailed review, vehicle-specific research, supplemental documentation, or formal written repair analysis is required, Peak Country offers a Comprehensive Estimate Review for $149. The service and price are explained before any paid work is performed.'],
  ['Do I need to understand the insurance process before contacting Peak Country?', 'No. You can start with a Free Initial Assessment. There is no obligation or automatic charge simply for starting the conversation.'],
  ['What is Peak Country’s role in an insurance-related repair?', 'Peak Country’s focus is the vehicle and the repair. We evaluate the damage, research the repair requirements, document what is needed, and explain the options so the customer has clear information to make an informed decision. Evaluate the vehicle. Research the repair. Document what is needed. Explain the options clearly.'],
];
const localFaqs = [
  ['What is a Free Initial Assessment?', 'A Free Initial Assessment is the first no-obligation conversation about the vehicle, visible damage, photos when useful, and the appropriate next step. It does not automatically include a physical panel-by-panel hail inspection, formal written estimate, or repair authorization.'],
  ['What happens after I use Get Started?', 'Peak Country reviews the information you provide and responds through the contact method supplied. The next step may be a conversation, a request for useful photos, scheduling an appropriate direct inspection, or explaining when professional estimating and documentation would help. Submitting the form does not authorize repair or create a charge.'],
  ['What is a professional hail inspection?', 'A professional hail inspection is a direct review of a clean vehicle under appropriate PDR lighting. The technician checks panels for dent patterns, paint condition, repairability, access, and possible disassembly needs. It is separate from the initial no-obligation conversation and is arranged when appropriate.'],
  ['Does Peak Country repair door dings?', 'Yes, Peak Country evaluates parking-lot door dings, minor dents, and repairable creases for PDR. Paint condition, depth, body-line involvement, and access determine whether a paintless approach makes sense.'],
  ['What areas does Peak Country serve?', 'Peak Country is a mobile service based around Greeley and serving Weld County and nearby Northern Colorado communities by appointment. Location and suitable working conditions are confirmed when scheduling.'],
  ['How much PDR experience does Peak Country have?', 'Peak Country is built around 20+ years of hands-on Paintless Dent Repair experience. That experience supports repairability decisions, access planning, controlled metal work, and realistic customer communication.'],
  ['Does a nearby NOAA hail report prove my vehicle was damaged?', 'No, a nearby NOAA or Storm Prediction Center report does not prove that a specific vehicle was struck or damaged. Reports describe observations at a place and time and may have limited coverage or location precision.'],
  ['What size hail can damage a vehicle?', 'There is no single hail size that predicts damage to every vehicle. Diameter matters, but wind, impact angle, storm duration, vehicle exposure, panel shape, material, and finish condition also affect the outcome.'],
  ['What happens when trim or a headliner needs to be removed for PDR?', 'Approved components may be handled or removed carefully when necessary to create safe access behind a panel. Reassembly includes attention to applicable clips, fasteners, connections, trim fit, and the surrounding area.'],
];
const faqs = [...repairFaqs, ...insuranceFaqs, ...localFaqs];
const pageSchema = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', '@id': `${site.url}/faq/#faq`, mainEntity: faqs.map(([name, text]) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text } })) };
---
<Layout title={title} description={description} exactTitle pageSchema={pageSchema}>
  <header class="page-head faq-head"><div class="eyebrow">Straight answers from a technician-led service</div><h1>Paintless Dent Repair and Auto Hail Repair FAQ</h1><p>Practical answers about repairability, factory paint, inspections, insurance-related repairs, mobile service, and Northern Colorado hail information.</p></header>
  <section class="section faq-directory"><nav aria-label="FAQ topics"><a href="#pdr-questions">PDR &amp; repairability</a><a href="#insurance-questions">Insurance &amp; documentation</a><a href="#warranty">Workmanship warranty</a><a href="#local-questions">Local service &amp; hail data</a></nav></section>
  <article class="section faq-expanded">
    <section id="pdr-questions"><div class="eyebrow">PDR &amp; Repairability</div><h2>How paintless repair works</h2><div class="faq-list">{repairFaqs.map(([question, answer]) => <details><summary>{question}</summary><p>{answer}</p></details>)}</div></section>
    <section id="insurance-questions"><div class="eyebrow">Insurance &amp; Documentation</div><h2>The vehicle and the repair come first.</h2><p class="direct-answer">Peak Country provides repair-focused evaluation, research, documentation, and communication. Insurance coverage, reimbursement, deductibles, limitations, and exclusions remain subject to the customer’s individual policy.</p><div class="faq-list">{insuranceFaqs.map(([question, answer]) => <details><summary>{question}</summary><p>{answer}</p></details>)}</div></section>
    <section id="warranty"><div class="eyebrow">Workmanship warranty</div><h2>How does the workmanship warranty work?</h2><p class="direct-answer">Qualifying Peak Country PDR workmanship is covered for as long as the original customer owns the vehicle, subject to the repair-specific warranty terms, limitations, and exclusions supplied with the qualifying repair.</p><p><a href="/warranty/">Read the Limited Lifetime Workmanship Warranty summary →</a></p></section>
    <section id="local-questions"><div class="eyebrow">Local Service &amp; Hail Data</div><h2>Peak Country and Northern Colorado</h2><div class="faq-list">{localFaqs.map(([question, answer]) => <details><summary>{question}</summary><p>{answer}</p></details>)}</div></section>
  </article>
  <section class="section faq-links"><a href="/estimating-documentation/">Professional Estimating &amp; Documentation →</a><a href="/warranty/">Workmanship Warranty →</a><a href="/repair-standards/">PDR Repair Standards →</a><a href="/free-hail-inspection/">Free Initial Assessment →</a></section>
</Layout>
